  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three main floating support concepts for offshore wind turbines and highlights how each is stabilized in deep water. In all cases, the turbine itself has the same basic parts—blades, nacelle, and tower—but the underwater foundation and mooring approach differ.\n\nOne design is a spar, shown as a long, narrow floating column extending deep below the surface. It is held in place by angled mooring lines connected to seabed anchors. A second design is a semisubmersible, which uses several buoyant columns connected by a frame near the surface; it is also kept in position by spread moorings tied to anchors on the seafloor. The third design is a tension-leg platform, which appears to have a more compact floating base linked to the seabed by nearly vertical taut tendons attached to vertical-load anchors.\n\nAs a whole, the figure emphasizes the structural differences between these floating wind substructures and the distinct ways they maintain stability: deep draft for the spar, distributed buoyancy for the semisubmersible, and tensioned vertical restraints for the tension-leg platform.",
